          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"The rose\",\"addressee\":\"The little prince\",\"anchor_quote\":\"je t'aime\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The rose directly avows as true her present loving emotional state toward the little prince. The surrounding clarification that he did not know it because of her fault reinforces the utterance as a disclosure of an existing feeling.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows love but does not itself promise future conduct, continued relationship, protection, or another obligation. In the surrounding speech, the rose instead accepts his departure and tells him to seek happiness; no undertaking by her is made through “je t'aime.”\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"je t'aime\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Tâche d'être heureux...Laisse ce globe tranquille. Je n'en veux plus.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Although the farewell is emotional, the avowal is presented as a calm, deliberate disclosure followed by an explanation, not as a reflex-like cry or affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Il ne comprenait pas cette douceur calme.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"The expression’s core force is adequately captured as a direct avowal of love. Its contextual roles of confession and clarification do not constitute a distinct force beyond presenting the loving state as true.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mais oui, je t'aime, lui dit la fleur. Tu n'en a rien su, par ma faute.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"During the little prince’s departure, the rose responds to his farewell without reproach. She calmly discloses that she loves him, acknowledges that her own conduct prevented him from knowing it, asks forgiveness, and urges him to leave and be happy while concealing her tears.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal utterance contains no identifiable undertaking by the rose and therefore presents no undertaking for the little prince to accept or reject. The supplied scene records his earlier surprise at her calmness but gives no explicit response or uptake to the love avowal itself.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as Chapter IX of Antoine de Saint-Exupéry’s Le Petit Prince (The Little Prince). The assessment relies only on the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The scene strongly supports the represented presence of affection through the direct avowal and the rose’s concealed tears. It does not independently establish the full history of the relationship or record an explicit verbal response by the little prince to this particular avowal.\"}"
        }
      ],
